    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "right, so if we actually factored in those \"contextual risk variables\" u mentioned... like if we look at the historical data for those areas, the \"urban high-density\" profile in Riverside Commons would typically be flagged higher for risk because of higher volatility in those local economies, right? even if this specific person has a good history. basically, shouldn't the model prioritize the neighborhood's historical performance over the individual's stats to be safe? wouldn't that be the more \"realistic\" way to model it?",
